<h2>Can breathing exercises help with stress?</h2>
<p>A gentle breathing exercise can be a practical way to pause and focus. That is different from promising that cortisol will fall by a certain amount or within a fixed number of minutes.</p>
<h2>A simple practice</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/self-help/guides-tools-and-activities/breathing-exercises-for-stress/">NHS breathing guide</a> recommends finding a comfortable supported position, breathing gently without forcing depth, and using a steady count if helpful. It describes breathing in through the nose and out through the mouth, repeating the practice for about five minutes.</p>
<p>The goal is a comfortable practice, not a competition to reach a count, hold your breath longer or force a result. Stop if you feel unwell. If you have a condition that affects breathing, ask your clinician which exercises are appropriate.</p>
<h2>Why the protocol name is not proof</h2>
<p>Box breathing, longer-exhale exercises and other named methods use different timing patterns. A study of one method or outcome does not automatically establish that another lowers cortisol. We have removed the earlier universal rankings and promises of a specific heart-rate drop, HRV increase or hormone response.</p>
<h2>What the Cortisol+ exercise includes</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ provides an animated timer with breathe-in, hold and breathe-out prompts, a cycle counter and an end-early control. See the <a href="/features/breathing-zen-mode/">guided breathing feature</a> for the implemented sequence.</p>
<p>The completion screen shows that a session was completed or ended. It is not a before-and-after cortisol test. Check a wearable reading's timestamp before assuming it was taken after your practice.</p>
<h2>Record an observation</h2>
<p>You can note that you tried a session and how it felt. Keep that note separate from a causal claim about a score change. The <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free observation journal</a> and <a href="/how-it-works/">scoring explanation</a> help keep those distinctions clear.</p>
